From 1cf4c65f4c2c318e7a68707f1890fb11154b59dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: superc <superc@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 20:26:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/10] superc: multi-level package names and 'pkg.member' access

A package name is purely lexical: 'acme.util' and the nested spelling
'package acme { package util { ... } }' both compose the prefix
'acme__util', so a symbol declared inside becomes 'acme__util__sym'.
Nothing looks at directories or file names, and a dotted import does
not open anything either.  Nesting composes instead of being rejected,
which also fixes 'package a;' followed by an imported header carrying
'package b { ... }' being reported as a nested package.

'pkg.member' (any number of leading segments) resolves to the mangled
symbol and is accepted only for a package that was imported before the
use, or for the package the code is currently inside of.  The package
part is grown one segment at a time, so 'pkg.obj.field' reaches the
package global and hands the trailing '.field' back to the normal
postfix code.  A visible identifier keeps plain C member-access
semantics, diagnostics use the dotted spelling from the source.

Tests: 166 'pkg.member', 167 diagnostics, 168 across translation units,
172 an extension unit against a plain-C unit, 173 multi-level packages.
tests2 149/149 with TCC_EXT.
